"""WM host/OS seam — the ONE interface between window-manager logic and the machine under it.

PORTABILITY CONTRACT
    If Simple 2D (rendering surface + event delivery) runs on a platform, then
    the WM runs on that platform. Nothing else is a precondition.

That contract is the whole design rule for this file. The seam is therefore
exactly a 2D surface plus an event source, and nothing else. Every method below
had to answer "does the WM literally fail to run without this?" with yes.

    surface_acquire  — without a drawing target there is no window manager.
    surface_present  — without handoff nothing ever reaches the screen.
    surface_release  — the paired teardown; a WM that cannot give the surface
                       back cannot restart or hand over.
    event_poll       — without an event source the WM cannot respond to anything.

Four methods, because every platform has to implement them.

THIS FILE CONTAINS NO PLATFORM BACKEND, AND SAYS SO
  There are exactly two implementations here, and neither one is a platform:

    WmHost2dReference    — an in-memory reference implementation. It makes ZERO
                           OS calls and declares ZERO externs. It exists to pin
                           the contract and to run the conformance check. It is
                           NOT a Linux backend, not a FreeBSD backend and not a
                           SimpleOS backend, and it is never named as one.
    WmHost2dUnavailable  — the honest refusal, carrying the platform name and
                           the reason. Every real platform currently maps here.

  An earlier draft of this file did the wrong thing: it shipped classes called
  `WmHost2dLinux` / `WmHost2dFreeBsd` / `WmHost2dSimpleOs` whose bodies were the
  same in-memory bookkeeping, so three "platforms" reported success while
  touching no OS at all. The platform audit
  (doc/04_architecture/ui/wm_host_platform_matrix.md, cluster 1) is right that
  this is the worst artifact shape in the codebase. A capability is backed by a
  real implementation or it reports FALSE — there is no third option, and
  naming a struct after a platform does not constitute backing it.

WHERE THE REAL BACKENDS ARE
  They already exist and are correct; they are merely unreachable, because no
  dispatcher selects them (`hosted_backend.spl:257-274` has no callers and calls
  `.create` on types that only define `try_create`). Wiring that dispatch is a
  separate lane's job, so this file deliberately does not touch
  `hosted_backend.spl` or `host_compositor_bootstrap.spl`.

    macos     — real: src/runtime/hosted_cocoa.c (NSWindow/NSImageView),
                wrapper hosted_backend_cocoa.spl `try_create`.
    windows   — real: src/runtime/hosted_win32.c (RegisterClassExW /
                CreateDIBSection / BitBlt), wrapper hosted_backend_win32.spl.
    linux     — SDL2 backend only. The audit's anchored search finds ZERO
                occurrences of XOpenDisplay / XCreateWindow / XNextEvent /
                wl_display_connect / wl_compositor in non-vendored source, so
                there is no native X11 or Wayland backing to route to.
    freebsd   — no backend found. The FreeBSD VM harness boots (verified via
                scripts/check/check-freebsd-bootstrap-qemu.shs --smoke), but a
                2D backend for it does not exist.
    simpleos  — ROUTED, and proven on real firmware.
                src/os/services/wm/wm_host_2d_simpleos.spl implements this trait
                over the same `FramebufferDriver` that src/os/compositor/
                fb_backend.spl renders through (hardware LFB writes, readback
                via `read_pixel` -> `mmio_read32`) and over the PS/2 controller
                for events. Reach it through `wm_host_2d_for_backed` below.

  `wm_host_2d_for` takes a platform name and nothing else, so it has no surface
  to hand out, and still returns the honest refusal for every platform —
  simpleos included. That is not a placeholder; it is the truth about a call
  with no framebuffer in it. `wm_host_2d_for_backed` is the arm that can say
  yes, and it says yes only to a backend that declares real backing.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* IN THIS SEAM
Each is a real host/OS call somewhere in the WM tree, kept out because the WM
still boots and runs without it. Putting any of them here would silently add a
precondition and break the contract above. (These are the audit's Bucket C.)

  * clipboard      — src/lib/nogc_async_mut/wm/compositor.spl keeps clipboard
                     text as plain WM state. SimpleOS has no system-wide
                     clipboard service (clipboard is per web session, see
                     src/os/compositor/compositor.spl `session.clipboard`).
  * cursor shape   — the WM draws its own cursor into the surface it already
                     owns. Not a separate OS facility.
  * wall clock     — src/lib/nogc_async_mut/wm/wm_optimization.spl calls
                     `rt_time_now_micros()` for frame budgeting. A real host/OS
                     call, NOT expressible as 2D-or-event, but an optimisation
                     input: the WM runs without it. Named rather than absorbed.
                     Deliberately absent from this seam — an earlier draft
                     carried a `now_micros` that was frozen at 0, which made its
                     own health check pass on `0 >= 0`.
  * display enumeration — the screen's size is a property of the surface the WM
                     acquires, not a separate capability to query.
  * process exec / foreign windows — src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/play/wm/mod.spl
                     shells out to xdotool / import / osascript / PowerShell to
                     drive *foreign* windows. That is desktop automation of
                     another window system, not the WM running.

THE ONE CALL THAT DOES NOT FIT
  src/os/services/wm/wm_service.spl drives client apps over kernel IPC
  (`SYS_IPC_CREATE_PORT` 22, `SYS_IPC_RECV` 21, and `_ipc_send` in
  wm_codec.spl). Client IPC is a genuine host/OS dependency that is NOT
  expressible as 2D-or-event: an inbound client request can be modelled as an
  event, but the WM's *reply* to that client cannot. Reported rather than folded
  in, because widening the seam to absorb it would make kernel IPC a second
  precondition and break the portability contract.

REUSES THE EXISTING VOCABULARY
  `src/lib/common/window_protocol/window_protocol.spl` is a vocabulary, not an
  interface — it declares `WmInputEvent` and request structs but no behaviour,
  so there was no interface to consolidate into. This file adds only the
  behaviour and reuses that vocabulary: `event_poll` returns the canonical
  `WmInputEvent`, the same type `src/os/services/wm/wm_service.spl` speaks. No
  parallel event type is introduced.

TIER OWNERSHIP
  Owned by `nogc_async_mut` only — the tier that already holds the WM core
  (compositor.spl, input.spl, service.spl), which the audit confirms is already
  portable (zero `use`, zero `extern`). Those three files are not touched. The
  other three tiers get no copy; the four `play/wm` tiers are NOT equivalent
  (three are 3-11 line stubs), so replicating into them would be replicating
  into stubs. If a tier ever needs the seam it re-exports.
"""

use std.common.window_protocol.window_protocol.{WmInputEvent}
use std.common.window_protocol.geometry.{WindowId, Point, Size}

# ============================================================================
# Seam types
# ============================================================================

class WmHostStatus:
    ok: bool
    state: text

class WmHostSurface:
    ok: bool
    state: text
    id: u64
    width: u32
    height: u32

class WmHostEventPoll:
    """Poll status wrapped around the canonical `window_protocol.WmInputEvent`.

    The wrapper exists only to carry `ok`/`state`; the event itself is the
    shared vocabulary type, not a parallel one.
    """
    ok: bool
    state: text
    event: WmInputEvent

fn wm_host_status(ok: bool, state: text) -> WmHostStatus:
    WmHostStatus(ok: ok, state: state)

fn wm_host_no_surface(state: text) -> WmHostSurface:
    WmHostSurface(ok: false, state: state, id: 0, width: 0, height: 0)

fn wm_host_no_event(state: text) -> WmHostEventPoll:
    WmHostEventPoll(ok: false, state: state, event: WmInputEvent.mouse_move(WindowId(value: 0u64), Point.xy(0, 0)))

# ============================================================================
# The seam — 2D surface + event source. Four methods. Nothing else.
# ============================================================================

trait WmHost2d:
    me surface_acquire(width: u32, height: u32) -> WmHostSurface
    me surface_present(surface_id: u64, byte_len: i64) -> WmHostStatus
    me surface_release(surface_id: u64) -> WmHostStatus
    me event_poll() -> WmHostEventPoll

# ============================================================================
# Reference implementation — in memory, ZERO OS calls, and never called a platform
# ============================================================================

class WmHost2dReference:
    """In-memory reference implementation of the seam.

    Makes no OS calls and declares no externs. Its purpose is to pin the
    contract and to let the conformance check run the real WM frame logic
    without a display. It must never be presented as a platform backend, and
    `wm_host_2d_for` never returns it for a platform name.
    """
    screen_width: u32
    screen_height: u32
    surface_ctr: u64
    live_surface: u64
    present_count: i64
    pending: [WmInputEvent]

pub fn wm_host_2d_reference(screen: Size, pending: [WmInputEvent]) -> WmHost2dReference:
    WmHost2dReference(
        screen_width: screen.width,
        screen_height: screen.height,
        surface_ctr: 0,
        live_surface: 0,
        present_count: 0,
        pending: pending
    )

impl WmHost2d for WmHost2dReference:
    me surface_acquire(width: u32, height: u32) -> WmHostSurface:
        if width == 0 or height == 0:
            return wm_host_no_surface("invalid-size")
        if self.screen_width == 0 or self.screen_height == 0:
            return wm_host_no_surface("no-display")
        self.surface_ctr = self.surface_ctr + (1 as u64)
        self.live_surface = self.surface_ctr
        val w = if width > self.screen_width: self.screen_width else width
        val h = if height > self.screen_height: self.screen_height else height
        WmHostSurface(ok: true, state: "acquired", id: self.live_surface, width: w, height: h)

    me surface_present(surface_id: u64, byte_len: i64) -> WmHostStatus:
        if surface_id == 0 or surface_id != self.live_surface:
            return wm_host_status(false, "unknown-surface")
        if byte_len <= 0:
            return wm_host_status(false, "empty-frame")
        self.present_count = self.present_count + 1
        wm_host_status(true, "presented")

    me surface_release(surface_id: u64) -> WmHostStatus:
        if surface_id == 0 or surface_id != self.live_surface:
            return wm_host_status(false, "unknown-surface")
        self.live_surface = 0
        wm_host_status(true, "released")

    me event_poll() -> WmHostEventPoll:
        if self.pending.len() == 0:
            return wm_host_no_event("queue-empty")
        val head = self.pending[0]
        var rest: [WmInputEvent] = []
        var i: i64 = 1
        while i < self.pending.len():
            rest.push(self.pending[i])
            i = i + 1
        self.pending = rest
        WmHostEventPoll(ok: true, state: "event", event: head)

# ============================================================================
# Honest refusal — every real platform maps here until dispatch is wired
# ============================================================================

class WmHost2dUnavailable:
    """Refuses every seam call, by platform name and reason.

    This is the correct shape for a platform whose backend this repo cannot
    reach or cannot verify. It can never produce a green frame, so nothing
    downstream can mistake it for a working backend.
    """
    platform: text
    reason: text

pub fn wm_host_2d_unavailable(platform: text, reason: text) -> WmHost2dUnavailable:
    WmHost2dUnavailable(platform: platform, reason: reason)

fn _refusal(host: WmHost2dUnavailable) -> text:
    "unavailable:" + host.platform + ": " + host.reason

impl WmHost2d for WmHost2dUnavailable:
    me surface_acquire(width: u32, height: u32) -> WmHostSurface:
        wm_host_no_surface(_refusal(self))
    me surface_present(surface_id: u64, byte_len: i64) -> WmHostStatus:
        wm_host_status(false, _refusal(self))
    me surface_release(surface_id: u64) -> WmHostStatus:
        wm_host_status(false, _refusal(self))
    me event_poll() -> WmHostEventPoll:
        wm_host_no_event(_refusal(self))

# ============================================================================
# Platform routing — the tripwire
# ============================================================================

pub fn wm_host_2d_for(platform: text) -> WmHost2dUnavailable:
    """The seam's platform routing table.

    Every arm currently refuses, because no real backend is reachable yet. Each
    reason states what exists and what is missing, so wiring one is a one-line
    change here plus a flipped assertion in the conformance check.
    """
    if platform == "macos":
        return wm_host_2d_unavailable("macos", "hosted_cocoa.c backend is real but unreachable — no dispatcher calls it")
    if platform == "windows":
        return wm_host_2d_unavailable("windows", "hosted_win32.c backend is real but unreachable — no dispatcher calls it")
    if platform == "linux":
        return wm_host_2d_unavailable("linux", "SDL2 backend unreachable; no native X11 or Wayland backend exists in non-vendored source")
    if platform == "freebsd":
        return wm_host_2d_unavailable("freebsd", "VM harness boots but no 2D backend exists for this platform")
    if platform == "simpleos":
        return wm_host_2d_unavailable("simpleos", "backend is routed (os/services/wm/wm_host_2d_simpleos.spl) but this call supplies no framebuffer — use wm_host_2d_for_backed")
    wm_host_2d_unavailable(platform, "unknown platform")

pub fn wm_host_2d_for_backed(platform: text, backed: WmHost2d, backing_kind: text) -> WmHost2d:
    """Routing for a platform that has handed in a real, bound backend.

    `backing_kind` is the backend's own statement of what is under it —
    `"none"` when it cannot draw. A backend reporting `"none"` is refused here
    rather than passed through, so a struct named after a platform still cannot
    produce a green frame without something real behind it. Same honesty rule
    as the rest of this file, applied at the routing edge.

    Only `simpleos` is accepted today, because it is the only platform whose
    backend this repo can both reach and boot (QEMU under OVMF pflash — see
    scripts/check/check-simpleos-wm-host-seam-evidence.shs, which boots the
    real firmware, runs `wm_host_2d_frame` unmodified over the SimpleOS
    backend, and reads the resulting pixels back out of the framebuffer). The
    other four fall through to the refusal `wm_host_2d_for` already gives even
    if a caller hands one in — being handed an object is not evidence.
    """
    if backing_kind == "none":
        return wm_host_2d_unavailable(platform, "backend supplied but reports no real backing")
    if platform == "simpleos":
        return backed
    wm_host_2d_for(platform)

# ============================================================================
# The WM's host-facing frame step — the single consumer of the seam
# ============================================================================

class WmHostFrameReport:
    steps: text
    surface_ok: bool
    surface_state: text
    surface_width: u32
    surface_height: u32
    present_ok: bool
    events_drained: i64
    event_trace: text
    released_ok: bool

pub fn wm_host_2d_frame(host: WmHost2d, request: Size) -> WmHostFrameReport:
    """One WM frame expressed only through the 2D+event seam.

    Every implementation runs this identical logic — there is no platform
    branch anywhere in it. That is the portability contract in code: whatever
    satisfies the four-method seam is sufficient for the WM to run.
    """
    # A frame is exactly the requested surface area at 4 bytes per pixel; the
    # WM does not get to declare a frame size independent of its surface.
    val byte_len = (request.width as i64) * (request.height as i64) * 4
    var steps = ""
    val surface = host.surface_acquire(request.width, request.height)
    steps = steps + "acquire(" + surface.state + ")"

    if not surface.ok:
        return WmHostFrameReport(
            steps: steps,
            surface_ok: false,
            surface_state: surface.state,
            surface_width: 0,
            surface_height: 0,
            present_ok: false,
            events_drained: 0,
            event_trace: "",
            released_ok: false
        )

    val presented = host.surface_present(surface.id, byte_len)
    steps = steps + " present(" + presented.state + ")"

    var drained: i64 = 0
    var trace = ""
    var polling = true
    while polling:
        val poll = host.event_poll()
        if not poll.ok:
            polling = false
        else:
            drained = drained + 1
            val e = poll.event
            trace = trace + str(e.window_id.value) + "/" + str(e.key_code.code) + "/" + str(e.point.x) + "," + str(e.point.y) + ";"
    steps = steps + " events(" + str(drained) + ")"

    val released = host.surface_release(surface.id)
    steps = steps + " release(" + released.state + ")"

    WmHostFrameReport(
        steps: steps,
        surface_ok: surface.ok,
        surface_state: surface.state,
        surface_width: surface.width,
        surface_height: surface.height,
        present_ok: presented.ok,
        events_drained: drained,
        event_trace: trace,
        released_ok: released.ok
    )
